.\" Has no version string on purpose: the release bump touches eight files
.\" and this is not one of them. Keep it in step with HELP in the four CLIs.
.TH SHCL 1 2026-10-04 shcl "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shcl \- read, edit, validate and format Simple Hierarchical Config Language files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY shcl
.B get
.RI [ type ]
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE PATH
.YS
.SY shcl
.B set
.RB [ \-\-write | \-w ]
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE
.YS
.SY shcl
.B fmt
.RB [ \-\-write | \-w | \-\-check ]
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE
.YS
.SY shcl
.B check
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE
.YS
.SY shcl
.B init
.BI \-\-schema= SCHEMA
.RB [ \-\-no\-banner ]
.YS
.SY shcl
.BR count | instances
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE PATH
.YS
.SY shcl
.B children
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE
.RI [ PATH ]
.YS
.SY shcl
.B paths
.RI [ options ]
.I FILE
.YS
.SY shcl
.B migrate
.RB [ \-\-write | \-w | \-\-check ]
.RB [ \-\-lossy ]
.RB [ \-\-from\-2x ]
.I FILE
.YS
.SY shcl
.B tokens
.I FILE
.YS
.SY shcl
.B explain
.RI [ CODE ]
.YS
.SY shcl
.B help
.RI [ CMD ]
.YS
.SY shcl
.BR \-\-version | \-\-about | \-\-donate
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shcl
is the reference command for SHCL, the Simple Hierarchical Config Language.
Everything the language bindings do, it does from a shell: typed reads, edits,
schema validation, formatting, and starter-config generation. It is one
dependency-free executable, so it doubles as a config probe for shell scripts,
makefiles and CI jobs.
.PP
Values are stored as plain text and coerced only when the reading side asks for
a type, so a file can never silently decide that
.B NO
means false. A malformed line is skipped or repaired with a diagnostic rather
than taking down the whole file, which is why a read of an unrelated path still
answers after a typo three lines up.
.PP
Stable diagnostic codes go to stdout and the English prose to stderr, so a
script can match on
.B E014
without parsing a sentence.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B get
Read one value, or an array of them, at
.IR PATH .
The type is chosen at the call site with a type option; the default is
.BR \-\-string .
A trailing
.RB [ * ]
in a path segment fans the read across every instance of that segment.
.TP
.B set
Apply edits to
.IR FILE ,
the base document, and print the result \- or rewrite
.I FILE
in place with
.BR \-\-write ,
which creates
.I FILE
when nothing is there yet, giving it the same info block
.B init
writes unless
.BR \-\-no\-banner .
Lines the edits leave alone come back as they were written. With
.BR \-\-layer ,
or where the edited text would not load back the same, the whole document
comes out in canonical form, the way
.B fmt
writes it.
Edits go in as the repeatable
.BR \-\-set ,
.BR \-\-set\-literal ,
.BR \-\-set\-default ,
.B \-\-set\-literal\-default
and
.B \-\-remove
options. Raw blocks go in only as a write-ops script on stdin, which can make
the other edits too. See
.B WRITE OPS
below.
.TP
.B fmt
Print the canonical form of
.IR FILE ,
or rewrite it in place with
.BR \-\-write .
.B \-\-check
prints nothing and says by the exit code whether a rewrite would change the
file.
Comments and their placement survive. With
.B \-\-layer
it prints the merged document.
.TP
.B check
Load
.I FILE
and print its diagnostics. With
.BI \-\-schema= SCHEMA
it also validates the file against a schema, which is itself an ordinary
.B .shcl
file. Without it, a file that names its schema on a
.RB \(dq ##\ \ \ \ Schema\ \ \ PATH \(dq
comment line is validated against that, with a relative path read from the
file's own directory. A URL there is left to editors and not fetched.
Each diagnostic is one stdout line that starts with the word
.B line
and a number, with its prose on stderr. The last stdout line is a summary.
Exits 6 when
anything was found at error severity.
.TP
.B init
Print a commented starter config generated from a schema: required fields live,
optional ones commented out, wildcard paths noted. Prose it writes is prefixed
with two hashes and a commented-out setting with one; that is a convention, not
a rule. The info block naming the format
and pointing at its spec is left out by
.BR \-\-no\-banner .
.TP
.B count
Print the number of instances at
.IR PATH .
.TP
.B instances
Print the instance values at
.IR PATH ,
one per line. A value holding a line break is printed in its quoted escaped
spelling, so one instance is always one line.
.TP
.B children
Print the child field names under
.IR PATH ,
one per line, in file order and with duplicates kept. With
.I PATH
left out, the top level. Each name is printed in the form a path accepts, so a
name holding a dot or a quote can be spliced straight back into a lookup path.
A path that resolves to several instances lists the children of each in turn.
A path that does not resolve and a node with no children both print nothing
and exit 0; ask
.B count
when the difference matters.
.TP
.B paths
Print every field path in the document, one per line, in file order and
deduplicated \- the whole-document counterpart of
.BR children .
.TP
.B migrate
Rewrite a file written for shcl 2.x so the current parser reads the same
tree, and print it (or rewrite
.I FILE
with
.BR \-\-write ,
through the same gate as
.BR fmt ).
Only what the two rule sets read differently is touched: a backslash 2.x read
as an escape outside double quotes, a quote that never closed, and the
.B name:[disc]
selector sugar. Comments, blank lines, raw bodies and layout come through as
written. The migrated text's diagnostics are printed, so a line the new rules
refuse is named rather than silently kept.
Which file it is looking at cannot be read off the text, so the info block's
.B Format
line decides: a file that has it is left alone, and one without it keeps the
spellings the two rule sets read differently and exits 7 unless
.B \-\-from\-2x
says it really is 2.x. A rewritten file is stamped with that line, and
.B \-\-write
says how many lines it rewrote.
Before it saves a file with a rewritten line,
.B \-\-write
copies the original beside it, with
.B _old_v2
before the extension:
.B config.shcl
keeps its old bytes in
.BR config_old_v2.shcl .
It refuses at exit 8 and writes nothing when that name is taken. A file that
only gains the Format line gets no copy.
.B \-\-check
prints nothing and names each line a rewrite would change.
.TP
.B tokens
Print each line's lexical spans, one line of output per input line: the indent
length, then every name, selector, separator, value, element and comment as
.IR kind = start - end
byte offsets from the first character after the indent, with a mark for how a
piece was quoted, and the fault that made a line malformed. This is the view
the parser reads a line through, for seeing why it read a line as it did.
Each line is read on its own, so a raw body line is tokenized as if it were a
field line.
.TP
.B explain
Print what a diagnostic code means: the code, its severity and the rule behind
it. With no
.I CODE
it lists every code one line each. The codes are the portable half of a
diagnostic \- the same problem gets the same code in every binding \- so
this is where to look one up. The prose beside a code on screen is per-binding
voice and says the same thing at less length.
.TP
.BR help ", " \-\-version ", " \-\-about ", " \-\-donate
Print this command's help, its version, what SHCL is, or where to support it.
.B help
with a subcommand name prints that subcommand's usage and options alone, and
so does
.B \-\-help
after a subcommand.
.B help
is also
.BR \-h " or " \-\-help ,
and
.B \-\-version
is also
.BR \-v " or " \-V .
The flags are recognized anywhere in option position, after
.I FILE
included; only the value of a value-taking option and anything after
.B \-\-
are data. Several in one run each print once, in the order given, set off by
blank lines;
.B \-\-about
covers
.BR \-\-version ,
since it opens with the version line. A lone
.B \-\-version
prints its one line bare. A bare
.B shcl
with no arguments prints the help and exits 0.
.SH TYPE OPTIONS
Valid on
.B get
only. The default is
.BR \-\-string .
.TP
.BR \-\-int ", " \-\-float ", " \-\-bool ", " \-\-datetime ", " \-\-string ", " \-\-raw ", " \-\-rawinfo ", " \-\-duration ", " \-\-size
Read the value as that type.
.B \-\-rawinfo
reads a raw block's info-string \- the tag on its fence line \- rather than its
content.
.B \-\-duration
prints whole milliseconds and
.B \-\-size
whole bytes, from spellings such as
.BR 1h\ 30m ,
.B 500ms
and
.BR 1.5GiB .
A bare number takes its unit from a field name ending in one after a
.B \-
or
.BR _ ,
such as
.B timeout\-ms
or
.BR cache_mb ,
else from
.BR \-\-unit .
.TP
.B \-\-array
Read the value as an array of the chosen type, one element per line. As in
.BR instances ,
an element holding a line break is printed in its quoted escaped spelling.
.SH OPTIONS
The subcommands each option belongs to are named in parentheses. An option a
subcommand does not use is a usage error, not something quietly ignored.
.TP
.BI \-\-default= VALUE
.RB ( get )
Value to print when the read comes back anything but Good. Implies
.BR \-\-on\-bad=default ;
for arrays it is substituted per bad slot. An array read that resolved no slots
at all has none to substitute into, so the default prints once and reads exactly
like a one-element array that happens to equal it; ask
.B count
or add
.B \-\-slots
to tell the two apart. Giving an explicit
.B \-\-on\-bad
other than
.B default
alongside it is a usage error rather than a silent win for whichever came last.
.TP
.BR \-\-on\-bad= error | default | flag
.RB ( get )
What a failed read does.
.B error
fails loudly;
.B default
prints the default;
.B flag
prints the value anyway and reports through the exit code. The last is the
default.
.TP
.B \-\-slots
.RB ( get )
Prefix each output line with its slot status and a tab \- per element for an
array, per matched instance for a wildcard. A value holding a line break is
then printed in its quoted escaped spelling, so each slot stays one line.
.TP
.BI \-\-unit= UNIT
.RB ( get )
The unit a bare number is in when the field name gives none:
.BR ms ", " s ", " m ", " h " or " d
for
.BR \-\-duration ,
or
.BR B ", " kB ", " KB ", " MB ", " GB ", " TB ", " KiB ", " MiB ", " GiB " or " TiB
for
.BR \-\-size .
.TP
.B \-\-decimal
.RB ( get )
With
.BR \-\-size ,
read KB to TB as powers of 1000 rather than 1024. KiB to TiB are always 1024.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-banner
.RB ( init ", " "set \-\-write" " creating " FILE )
Leave out the info block naming the format and pointing at its spec.
.TP
.BR \-\-write ", " \-w
.RB ( fmt ", " set ", " migrate )
Rewrite
.I FILE
in place instead of printing to stdout. The write goes through a temp file and
a rename, so an interrupted run cannot truncate the config. On
.B set
a
.I FILE
that does not exist yet is created;
.B fmt
and
.B migrate
have nothing to read and report it missing.
.B migrate
also keeps the original file; see above.
.TP
.B \-\-lossy
.RB ( fmt ", " set ", " migrate )
With
.BR \-\-write ,
rewrite even when the load dropped lines this write would delete. Without it
the write refuses and nothing is changed.
.TP
.B \-\-from\-2x
.RB ( migrate )
The file really was written for 2.x, so rewrite the spellings the two rule
sets read differently. Without it those are left as written and
.B migrate
exits 7, because the same bytes mean one thing under each rule set and
nothing in the file says which one wrote it.
.TP
.B \-\-check
.RB ( fmt ", " migrate )
Print nothing. Exit 6 when
.B fmt
would change the file or
.B migrate
would rewrite a line, and 0 otherwise, so a script can ask which files in a
tree need formatting or migrating. The
.B Format
line a
.B migrate
rewrite adds is not a rewritten line, so a file that only lacks it is 0.
A rewrite the matching
.B \-\-write
would refuse is exit 7 instead, since
.B \-\-check
asks the same save gate.
.B migrate
also names on stderr each line it would change, as
.IR FILE : LINE .
It cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-write .
.TP
.BR \-\-strictness= loose | standard | strict
.RB ( get ", " set ", " fmt ", " check ", " count ", " instances ", " children ", " paths )
How much the loader tolerates; also written
.BR 1 ,
.B 2
and
.BR 3 .
Default
.BR standard .
.B init
takes no strictness because a schema is a program artifact and always loads at
standard.
.TP
.BI \-\-schema= SCHEMA
.RB ( check ", " init )
The schema file. On
.B check
it adds
.BR V ###
diagnostics, and wins over the file's own Schema line; on
.B init
it is what the starter config is generated from.
.TP
.BI \-\-layer= FILE
.RB ( get ", " fmt ", " count ", " instances ", " children ", " paths ", " set )
Merge a lower-priority layer under
.IR FILE .
Repeatable; earlier means lower priority.
.B check
takes no layers, because diagnostics cite line numbers and a merged document
has no single file to number against \- pipe
.B shcl fmt
into
.B shcl check
instead.
.TP
.BI \-\-set= PATH = VALUE
.RB ( get ", " fmt ", " count ", " instances ", " children ", " paths ", " set )
Override one path as the topmost layer, after every file. Repeatable. On
.B set
it is an edit to the document itself, so it persists with
.BR \-\-write .
.I VALUE
goes in as data: its type still follows the text, so
.B 8
is an int, but a comma or a quote in it is content rather than syntax.
.I PATH
ends at the first
.B =
outside quotes and brackets, so a selector may hold one.
.TP
.BI \-\-set\-literal= PATH = TEXT
.RB ( get ", " fmt ", " count ", " instances ", " children ", " paths ", " set )
As
.BR \-\-set ,
except
.I TEXT
goes in as value syntax the way a file writes it \- so
.B ports=80, 443
writes a two-element array. A
.B #
outside quotes ends the value, and text spanning lines is rejected.
.TP
.BI \-\-set\-default= PATH = VALUE
.TQ
.BI \-\-set\-literal\-default= PATH = TEXT
.RB ( get ", " fmt ", " count ", " instances ", " children ", " paths ", " set )
As
.B \-\-set
and
.BR \-\-set\-literal ,
except the write happens only when nothing is at the path yet \- the
write-out-defaults half of the writer.
.TP
.BI \-\-remove= PATH
.RB ( get ", " fmt ", " count ", " instances ", " children ", " paths ", " set )
Delete whatever is at
.IR PATH ,
with its subtree. Repeatable. Removing nothing is not an error, but a
.I PATH
that cannot parse is.
.PP
The five options above share one ordered list, so two of them touching the same
path resolve in the order given. Raw blocks still go in through the ops script.
.PP
Value options accept either spelling:
.BI \-\-default= VALUE
or
.BR "\-\-default " VALUE .
In the space form the next argument is taken as the value whatever it looks
like, so
.B \-\-default \-\-int
reads
.B \-\-int
as the default. Use
.B \-\-
to end the options when a
.I FILE
or
.I PATH
begins with a dash.
.PP
.I FILE
may be
.B \-
for stdin. With
.BR \-\-layer ,
.I FILE
is the highest file layer and each
.B \-\-layer
is merged under it in order;
.B \-\-set
applies last.
.PP
Some combinations are refused outright, each as a usage error.
.B \-\-write
with
.B \-\-layer
would fold the merged layers into the base file permanently.
.B \-\-write
with
.B \-\-set
outside
.B set
would persist what was given as a read-time override.
.B \-\-lossy
without
.B \-\-write
overrides nothing.
.B \-\-no\-banner
on
.B set
without
.B \-\-write
has no file to leave the info block out of.
.B \-\-check
with
.B \-\-write
asks for both answers at once.
.B \-\-write
with a
.I FILE
of
.B \-
has no file to rewrite.
.B \-\-default
with
.B \-\-on\-bad=error
or
.B \-\-on\-bad=flag
gives a value that neither mode would print.
.B \-\-layer=\-
is not valid on
.BR set ,
where stdin already has the ops script or the document.
.BR \-\-raw ,
.BR \-\-rawinfo ,
.B \-\-duration
and
.B \-\-size
have no
.B \-\-array
form. Two options that ask for different answers are refused whichever order
they came in, and the message names both: two different type options, such as
.B \-\-int
with
.BR \-\-raw ,
or one value option given two different values, such as
.B \-\-strictness=loose
with
.BR \-\-strictness=strict .
Repeating an option with the same value is allowed, and
.B \-\-layer
and
.B \-\-set
are ordered lists, so they repeat. And
.B \-
may be named only once across
.IR FILE ,
.B \-\-layer
and
.B \-\-schema
\- there is one stdin, so two names for it would each read part of a document.
.SH WRITE OPS
.B set
reads a write-ops script on stdin when none of
.BR \-\-set ,
.BR \-\-set\-literal ,
.BR \-\-set\-default ,
.B \-\-set\-literal\-default
or
.B \-\-remove
gives the edits. One op per line, fields separated by tabs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
int|float|bool|string|datetime<TAB>PATH<TAB>VALUE
<type>\-array<TAB>PATH<TAB>V1<TAB>V2...
<type>[\-array]\-default<TAB>...
literal[\-default]<TAB>PATH<TAB>TEXT
raw[\-default]<TAB>PATH<TAB>INFO<TAB>CONTENT
empty<TAB>PATH
comment<TAB>PATH<TAB>TEXT
clear\-comments<TAB>PATH
remove<TAB>PATH
banner<TAB>on|off
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B \-default
forms write only if the path is absent.
.B clear\-comments
takes off the comment lines above the node, all of them, so a comment can be
replaced instead of added to.
.B banner on
puts the info block at the end, taking off an old one first;
.B banner off
only takes it off. String, comment and raw values decode
.BR \en ,
.B \et
and
.BR \e\e ,
and a line starting with
.B #
is a script comment. A
.B raw
op's INFO may not hold a line break or a
.BR # ,
which would read back as a comment on the fence line.
.PP
.I FILE
given as
.B \-
follows stdin: it is the piped document when an option holds the edits, and an
empty base when the ops script has stdin instead.
.SH WRITING IN PLACE
Every subcommand that loads a document prints the load's diagnostics to stderr,
once per run, so a recovered-from typo is just as visible when the value reads
back fine and the result goes to stdout. An in-place write also refuses
outright, at exit 7, when the load dropped content the rewrite would delete
\- a typo does not cost the line above it.
.B \-\-lossy
is how to say it was meant.
.SH MIGRATING FROM 2.X
The 3.0 lexical rules are smaller than 2.x's, so a file written for 2.x can
read differently under them.
.B migrate
rewrites such a file so the current parser reads the tree 2.x read.
.PP
Most of the change is loud: a quote that never closes is
.BR E017 ,
bracket text after a colon is
.BR E019 ,
an unknown escape in double quotes is
.BR E023 ,
a Windows path in double quotes with a \et or \en escape is
.BR E024 ,
and a line the current rules cannot read at all is a malformed line. A file
with one of those says so the first time it loads.
.PP
The comment rule did not change: a
.B #
outside quotes opened a comment in 2.x and still does.
.PP
Which file it is looking at is the one thing
.B migrate
cannot read off the text: a backslash value is written the same way under both
rule sets and means two different things, so rewriting a 3.0 file would change
what it says. The info block has a
.B Format
line naming the format's major, and that decides. A file with it has
nothing to migrate. A file without it keeps those spellings as written and
exits 7, unless
.B \-\-from\-2x
says it really is 2.x. A file
.B migrate
rewrites is stamped with the line, so a second run has the answer the first
one did not. A raw block that never closes leaves the line nowhere to go, so
.B migrate
exits 7 and writes nothing until the block is closed.
.PP
Bracket text after the colon, and a line break in a value that starts like a
Windows path, are what 2.x bound that nothing binds now.
.B migrate
exits 7 on either rather than reporting success;
.B \-\-lossy
accepts the loss on a rewrite.
.PP
Three edges read differently, and
.B migrate
leaves all three as written. A fence label holding a
.B #
ran to the end of the line in 2.x and ends at the
.B #
now, with the rest as the line's comment; a label has no quoting, so rename it.
A carriage return in the middle of a line, at the edge of a piece, was content
in 2.x and is trimmed now. And an indent that matches no open level's column,
which 2.x placed by a looser comparison, is
.B E012
now; re-indent it by hand.
.B migrate \-\-write
exits 7 while the migrated text holds such a line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Good.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error.
.TP
.B 2
The value is empty.
.TP
.B 3
The path was not found.
.TP
.B 4
The value is not readable as the requested type.
.TP
.B 5
Multiple instances matched where one was wanted.
.TP
.B 6
.B check
found errors, a strict load failed,
.BR init 's
schema has faults, or
.B \-\-check
found a rewrite to make.
.TP
.B 7
An in-place write was refused because the load dropped content the rewrite
would delete
.RB ( \-\-lossy
overrides), or
.B migrate
left something behind: values it cannot decide between the two rule sets
.RB ( \-\-from\-2x
decides them), a 2.x binding with no spelling here, a line whose indent
matches no open level, or a raw block that never closes.
.TP
.B 8
A file or stream could not be read or written \- it is missing, unreadable,
not a file, or the target cannot be written.
.SH EXAMPLES
Read an int, with a fallback when the field is absent:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shcl get \-\-int server.shcl workers
shcl get \-\-int \-\-default=8 server.shcl thread\-pool
.fi
.RE
.PP
Count repeated fields, then fan one read across every instance:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shcl count server.shcl site
shcl get \-\-array \-\-slots server.shcl 'site[*].tls.hsts'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Validate against a schema and gate a build on the exit code:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shcl check \-\-schema=app\-schema.shcl app.shcl
.fi
.RE
.PP
Generate a starter config for your own users:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shcl init \-\-schema=app\-schema.shcl > app.shcl
.fi
.RE
.PP
Normalize and edit a file in place:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shcl fmt \-\-write server.shcl
shcl set \-\-write server.shcl \-\-set 'workers=8'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Validate a merged document, which
.B check
will not do directly:
.PP
.RS
.nf
shcl fmt \-\-layer=base.shcl site.shcl |
    shcl check \-\-schema=s.shcl \-
.fi
.RE
